#!/bin/zsh
# Re-extracts poster frames from the aerial cache, rebuilds the
# solar-dynamic HEIC, and sets it as the desktop wallpaper. After this
# runs once, macOS rotates the wallpaper natively based on the sun's
# position at your current location — no daemon, no cron.
#
# Usage: tahoe-install [--pack <Name>]
#   --pack    Aerial pack name (default: Tahoe). The pack must contain
#             four phase-named assets ("<Pack> Morning|Day|Evening|Night").
#
# Re-run any time you (re)download aerial variants in System Settings.

set -euo pipefail

PACK="Tahoe"
while (( $# > 0 )); do
    case $1 in
        --pack) PACK=$2; shift 2 ;;
        *) print -u2 "unknown argument: $1"; exit 2 ;;
    esac
done

BIN_DIR="${0:A:h}"
HEIC="$HOME/Pictures/AerialWallpapers/${PACK}-Dynamic.heic"

echo "==> extracting poster frames from aerial cache (pack: $PACK)"
"$BIN_DIR/tahoe-extract-frames.swift" --pack "$PACK"

echo "==> building solar-dynamic HEIC"
"$BIN_DIR/tahoe-build-dynamic.swift" --pack "$PACK"

echo "==> setting as desktop wallpaper"
/usr/bin/osascript -e \
    "tell application \"System Events\" to tell every desktop to set picture to \"$HEIC\""

cat <<EOF

Done. macOS will now rotate the wallpaper based on the sun's altitude/azimuth
at your current location.

For travel-aware behavior, ensure Location Services is enabled:
  System Settings → Privacy & Security → Location Services → ON
  …→ System Services (bottom) → "Setting Time Zone": ON

If location is off, macOS falls back to the time zone's representative location
— still works, just less precise than Wi-Fi geolocation.
EOF
